>
    <p class="lede">
        A Canadian firm designs a product, grows locally, and then sells to a
        foreign multinational that already has global manufacturing and
        distribution.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Chariot Carriers, in Alberta, designed bicycle child trailers and
            accessories, including plastic wheels, hitches, and an attachment
            he called the wing. Acquired by Thule.
        </li>
        <li>
            Tenet Medical Engineering developed products for orthopaedic
            surgery. Acquired by Smith &amp; Nephew.
        </li>
        <li>
            Dynastream Innovations, of Cochrane, developed personal activity
            monitors. Acquired by Garmin.
        </li>
        <li>
            His history of the Auto Pact: in the early 1960s, Canadian plants
            made standard transmissions and the United States was moving to
            automatics. He said Canada put a 25 percent tariff on cars entering
            with automatic transmissions. The tariff fight led to the Auto
            Pact, later NAFTA, later CUSMA.
        </li>
        <li>
            Expo 67 is when Canadian design came of age, including the design organization later headquartered in Montreal,
            now the World Design Organization. By the 1990s, he said, China had
            become the world's primary manufacturer.
        </li>
        <li>
            His ask: a serious look at what scaling manufacturing and AI in
            Canada means on a 2080 horizon, from a Canadian point of view.
        </li>
    </ul>
</KickoffSession>
